  , decision "session-deployment"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.deployment"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Grammar.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Deployment.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Syntax.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "Grammar.deploymentBody is nonReservedSymbol \"deployment\" true (includeIdent, as requirements) then a name, of, the protocol and a block of deployItem, a recursive parser (as stmt) over kindDecl, groupDecl (outside { }, or island, datacenter, machine, replicated or sharded with a name, holding deployItems) and roleDecl (Role; or Role { ifaceDecl* }, ifaceDecl being scope name : kind;); every word inside is W (nonReservedSymbol), so kind, island and local stay names. Text.deploymentDecl reads it into DeploymentDef {name, protocol, world : List (Node String)}: built-in kinds by name (IfaceKind.call ... datagram), a kindDecl refining a known kind with IfaceKind.refine and its adds and removes as Conditions, failing at the syntax for an unknown or repeated kind, a group inside a role, outside or island below the top or anything else at the top, a repeated interface name of one role, and more than 256 nodes. Text.fileDefs accepts deploymentBody after its protocol (the of name must be a protocol defined earlier) into Acc.deployments. Deployment.messages walks a Global once (as Instances.mentions) collecting each message's sender, receiver, label, id and cause; Deployment.pairs groups them by sender and receiver in first-seen order and marks a pair a reply link when every one of its messages is a reply: its cause chain (ids looked up among the messages, at most 64 links) reaches a message from its receiver to its sender. Deployment.check d roles g answers a Report: placements (role, path, interfaces) from Topology.placed; an issue (subject role, text) for each role of P placed zero or several times and each placed name not a role; and per link the receiver's interfaces in block order, the first with Topology.reachable from the sender, its implicit name and the conditions of Topology.channel, or an issue naming each interface and why its scope refuses the sender; a reply link is then given the interface, conditions and verdict of the link from its receiver to its sender, with a note, and adds no issue of its own. Deployment.svg lays the tree out (top-level groups side by side, children stacked) and draws each link from box edge to box edge, a refused one dashed. Syntax.elabDeployment resolves P as elabSystem resolves an accepted protocol (unknown: a Lean error), evaluates it with evalProtocol, reads the block with Text.deploymentDecl and logs every issue of Deployment.check as an error at the first identifier of the block naming the issue's subject. SessionTools.deployment reads the text with Text.readText, takes the last DeploymentDef or the one named, builds its protocol's global as Instances.systemsOf does, and answers verdict placed or refused, reading, placements, links, issues, svg (theme dark or light) and provenance; MCP lists it as session_deployment."
      ["LeanFM/Session/DeploymentTests.lean: the Orders deployment places Client, Api, Db and Bank; Client to Api uses us.Api.ingress.https and adds hostile; the replies Api to Client, Db to Api and Bank to Api travel back over us.Api.ingress.https, us.Db.local.sql and eu.Bank.peering.api with those links' conditions; Api to Bank uses eu.Bank.peering.api and adds partition; Api to Db adds nothing; a declared grpc kind adds deadline exceeded; a sharded group adds per-key order; the protocol's session_check reading is the same with and without the deployment; the view draws every role and one arrow per link", "LeanFM/Session/DeploymentTests.lean: refused, each with its reason: Client sending to an internal or local interface; a role unplaced, placed twice, or not a role; an unknown kind and a kind declared twice; two interfaces with one name; an island inside a machine; a deployment of an unknown protocol; a refused link is dashed in the view", "LeanFM/Session/LeanSyntaxTests.lean: in a Lean file an unplaced role, a refused link and an unknown kind are Lean errors at the block, a placed deployment elaborates, and a definition named deployment or kind still compiles", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: MCP lists session_deployment"]
  , decision "session-diagram"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.diagram"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Diagram.lean"]
      "Global.diagram g roles theme title walks g once in a state monad holding the vertical cursor, the SVG elements and the ids of messages drawn so far with their sender, receiver, cause and whether each was a reply. Arrows are emitted only from msg; isReply walks the cause chain (at most 64 links) and a message reaching a drawn non-reply message from its receiver to its sender gets class msg reply and a dotted stroke (Shape.replyDash, stroke-dasharray 1 6 with round caps), every other message a solid one, while frame dividers keep their 6 4 dashes; SessionView.html draws a shape of class reply with round caps, and its event stream draws an event that answers a request dotted the same way. The where clause is a separate text of class guard under the arrow. A frame opening directly under another frame's tab starts lower so the tabs do not overlap. The label adds ' - route' and ' - status'. answers returns the request a reply answers; a request with an id opens an activation bar (request id, callee, inset = the callee's bars still open, top, bottom), its first reply closes it. A later reply extends the most recent segment when no divider follows its bottom; otherwise it adds a segment starting at the greater of branchTop and the previous segment top. This prevents repeated replies from creating a bar at the initial branchTop of zero or before their request. Bars are drawn under the arrows. act emits an action box. choice, parallel, join, loop, sessions and box each emit a frame (class frame alt, parallel, join, loop, sessions, ref) with a UML tab (alt, parallel, loop, ref) and a caption: the decider from Global.decider as 'R decides', or 'race: no single role decides'; 'any k of n'; the loop name; the included protocol's name. Branches are separated by dashed dividers; nested frames are inset. var emits 'continue N'. Every piece of protocol text passes through diagramEscape. Themes: dark (the default, a #111 background) and light. Arrows are drawn at Shape.arrowWidth (2.5) with arrowheads in user-space units; frameTop moves 14 below the last arrow (22 below a frame tab) and dividers and frame bottoms 12, so no frame edge or divider runs along an arrow. Diagram.columnWidth sizes the lifeline spacing from the longest Diagram.labelText among Diagram.messagesOf the protocol; a choice's caption is the decider Global.decider names."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SessionTests.lean: in the diagrams of Purchase, Checkout, Transfer and ReplicatedOrder every arrow is at least 6 pixels from every frame edge and divider; messages are drawn at stroke width 2.5 with arrowheads in user-space units", "LeanFM/Session/SessionTests.lean: Purchase draws one arrow per label, two dotted replies (Quote, SoldOut, stroke-dasharray 1 6 with round caps) and an alt divider still dashed 6 4 and one alt frame captioned Shop decides, with the guard after Pay; ReplicatedOrder draws a ref frame for Replicate around a join frame captioned any 2 of 3; Transfer draws a loop frame with continue Block; TransferWithTimer draws an action box and a race caption; a sessions block draws a sessions frame; PayThenCharge draws Quote, Hold and Receipt as replies and Order, Pay and Verify as calls; a guard with <, && and > is escaped and no raw < from it appears; RepeatedReplies draws one activation from its request through both replies and no header overlap; Checkout shows - POST /orders, - 201 and - 402 in labels and draws six activation bars; a route with <, & and > is escaped"]
  , decision "session-calls"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.calls"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Calls.lean"]
      "Global.messages lists every message once in written order (a choice copies its continuation into each branch, so a message is kept at its first appearance, keyed by id, label, roles and cause). Global.classify folds over them: a message answers the first request up its cause chain (at most 64 links) whose sender is its receiver and whose receiver is its sender. Calls.inDetail r m walks m's ancestors and is true when it meets r before any of r's responses; Calls.parent m is the nearest ancestor request whose detail holds m; Global.calls builds the tree from requests by parent; callsText prints two lines per call, 'id: Label  Src -> Dst  route' then '  returns R1 status | R2 status' (or 'returns no reply'), nested calls indented four spaces. Local.typestate walks the projection in StateM, allocating S0, S1, ... in order and printing methods per state (send as sendLabel(m : Label) : Next, receive as receive() : Label times Next, events as Label() : Next, offer as one receive with alternatives, parallel as fork, sessions as serve, a branch that does nothing as stop() : End); Typestate.norm turns a parallel with one live side into that side followed by the continuation. Global.typestates projects each listed role and returns its typestate or the projection error."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SessionTests.lean: Checkout lists two calls with routes and statuses and the bank's charge nested under the payment; Purchase lists Pay and Shipped with no reply and Paid nested under Pay; the client's and the bank's typestates of Checkout and the sender's of Transfer (continue returns to S0) are exact"]
  , decision "session-generative"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.generative", "clause:selfhost.session.generative.execution", "clause:selfhost.session.generative.monitor"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTypes.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "Extend the read-only protocol parser with annotation AST nodes and retain a core-erasing build. Store the original body beside Global in parsed tool protocols. Resolve inline and external annotations by unique named choices and message id (or unambiguous label); external entries replace matching inline entries explicitly and identify their origin. Use exact rational arithmetic, milliseconds and bounded finite path expansion (4096 paths, 200 statements, 16 distribution entries, existing 64 KiB and nesting bounds). Validate each path's clock/event scope and time monotonicity before sampling. Seeded selection draws integer tickets against exact common denominators. Emit ordinary Session.Event envelopes and separate obligation reports. Timed log monitoring replays timestamped prefixes against structural paths without requiring their sampled delay or probability. Check all annotations independently of Global.issues; preserve existing unannotated simulation and JSON protocols. No filesystem, network, external code execution, or credentials in this layer. Reject unsupported timed concurrency/recursion/includes with actionable errors. Exact distributions and stable metadata are returned as data, not inserted as HTML. The session view displays effective annotations and deadline reports through text nodes and retains the external overlay in editor calls."
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTests.lean: accepted example, erasure, external overlay equivalence/replacement, exact mass, deterministic generation and conform round trip; zero-weight branch retained; invalid fractions, sums, bounds, forward refs, duplicate ids, unsupported scheduling and backwards times rejected; deadline boundary, late, pending, missing times, wrong sender and separate sessions checked", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: session tools expose overlay and horizon arguments through MCP"]
  , decision "session-mdp"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Decision.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "Retain decision markers and sampled local weights during finite path expansion; schedule ignores markers. Build a prefix tree with explicit choice/action nodes, event/time state nodes and terminal reports. Preserve complete action/event identity in keys, rather than labels alone. Multiply local exact weights and verify against compiled path masses, then check the sum of unique terminal masses equals one and each nonterminal outgoing distribution equals one. Bound graphs to 96 nodes and dimensions/output to explicit limits. Sum violating terminal masses once regardless of obligation count. Generate DiagramModel shapes and SVG through the existing escaped renderer; the MCP view builds SVG and text nodes, never inserts returned markup. session_mdp uses ordinary tool dispatch and session view attachment, returns the original overlay and DesiredOutput provenance, and refuses unresolved policies or unsupported timed scheduling."
      ["LeanFM/Session/DecisionTests.lean: exact example masses and total, external replacement, zero branches, unknown weights, duplicate labels, multiple obligations, pending status, nesting, resource limits and SVG injection", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean and scripts/session_view_smoke.js: discovery, dispatch, SVG/shapes and native view rendering with overlay retention"]
  , decision "session-amounts"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.amounts"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTypes.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Decision.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "GenerativeTypes adds Amount (unit and a list of quantity and probability outcomes) and Penalty (quantity, unit, payee) on Obligation. Text parses amount after status (one quantity, or sample of quantity-unit-probability entries checked by checkWeights, one unit, distinct quantities) into Stmt.valued, which build erases to its message like Stmt.timed, and pays N UNIT to Role after on breach record. Generative.expandStmt turns valued into one Action.transfer per outcome after the message action (sender, receiver, unit, quantity, weight), multiplying the path probability; timed keeps the actions after its message and multiplies too, so amount and time samples compose in either nesting. resolveStmt records amount metadata. schedule ignores transfers. Decision.steps folds a transfer into the preceding state step (key and detail carry the amount, weight multiplied) and gives the terminal its balances: per role and unit, received minus sent, plus each violated obligation's penalty, held as signed integers. Decision.expected sums terminal mass times balance with exact rationals kept as separate credit and debit masses, written with a sign. session_mdp returns balances per terminal, expectedBalances and expectedPenalty."
      ["LeanFM/Session/DecisionTests.lean: the priced deposit example gives exact terminal balances, conserved per unit, expected balances and expected penalty; amount samples multiply terminal masses and compose with time samples; erasure keeps the Global unchanged; invalid amounts (mixed units, bad sums, duplicate quantities, oversized, amount on an event, penalty to self or to an unknown role) are rejected; a pending obligation's penalty is not counted"]
  , decision "session-mdp-loops"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.loops"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Decision.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "Chain.build walks the resolved statement list backwards with a continuation state, in a state monad holding states and edges: a message, with its amount and time annotations peeled, adds a state whose one edge to the continuation carries the message, probability 1 and the amount's mean as an exact Rat; an event likewise; a weighted choice adds a choice state with one policy edge per branch to that branch's entry; rec allocates its state first, builds the body with the loop name bound to it and the rec's continuation, and adds a loop edge to the body's entry; continue returns the bound state; a clock is skipped; an obligation, parallel, join, sessions, include or unweighted choice is rejected. The end state is absorbing. Chain.solve builds (I - Q)^T over the transient states and solves for the start's unit vector by Gauss-Jordan elimination with exact Rat; a zero pivot means a closed set of transient states and is reported as a session that can loop forever. Traversals, balances and expected messages are sums over edges. The drawing lays states out in levels by breadth-first depth from the start; forward edges are straight arrows, edges back to a state no deeper than their source are curves to the right; edge labels sit on background plates. Decision draws a transfer on the edge into its message's state rather than in the state, and shows each non-terminal node's mass as its expected visits. session_mdp chooses the chain when the body contains rec or view is chain, and the tree otherwise."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: a retry loop gives exact visits, traversals and expected balances; a geometric loop with exit probability 1/4 visits its state 4 times; the priced example without its obligation gives the tree's expected balances; a loop with no way out, an obligation in a loop, parallel, an unweighted choice and too many states are rejected; the SVG escapes protocol text and draws back edges as paths"]
  , decision "session-mdp-rewards"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.rewards"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTypes.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "TimeExpr gains relative (after), which schedule bases on the previous event's time and Text writes back as after. Accrual (payer, payee, unit, quantity, periodMs) is a Stmt that build erases and expandStmt refuses with a pointer to the chain view. Chain.build gives every message and event edge the mean of its relative delay (an absolute time is noted when no accrual is present and refused when one is) and turns accrue into a zero-delay rate edge. Chain.regimes propagates the accruals in force from the start along edges in breadth-first order (a rate edge replaces the accrual with the same payer, payee and unit) and refuses a state reached with two different sets. Each edge into the single end is retargeted to an end state of its own, named after the edge, so outcomes stay apart. Chain.solveAbsorb solves (I - Q) h = r for each end with the same exact Gauss-Jordan elimination, giving the probability of each outcome from every state. An outcome's joint expectation of an edge reward is visits(src) x p x reward x h(dst); dividing by the outcome's probability gives the conditional value. Expected time, residence and accrued amounts are sums over edges of traversals x mean delay (x rate). session_mdp picks the chain when the body has rec or accrue. Chain.contributions sums traversals x amount per role, unit and source in protocol order; Chain.legend writes each role's units with their sources and, for exactly two units of opposite sign, the larger magnitude per the smaller; Chain.breakdownJson nests them exactly."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: a loan with a two-week grace at 0 USD per week and 10 USD per week after gives exact expected duration, interest and balances; per-outcome probabilities sum to 1 and conditional balances average back to the overall ones; the limit order splits into Filled and GaveUp outcomes of 1/2 each with 1004 and 4 USD; after delays drive the tree's times too; a rate that differs between merging paths, an absolute time with an accrual, an accrual in the tree view, a zero period, a rate to oneself and a negative rate are rejected"]
  , decision "session-mdp-walk"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.walk"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "Chain.walk keeps the current state and, for each step, searches breadth-first through choice, loop and rate edges (each state once) for message or event edges matching the step's label and, when given, sender and receiver; exactly one match is taken, with the path of silent edges to it, and the product of their probabilities. Counts per edge, visits per state and the time per state (the step's afterMs, or the edge's mean delay) accumulate as exact rationals; rewards apply the same per-traversal function as expectations (the trade vector, and each rate in force at the edge's source times the step's time). session_mdp with an events argument groups the stream by session (first appearance), turns timeMs or time into delays since the previous event of the session, walks each session and sums their balances, durations and counts; a walk argument walks one hand-written list. The result replaces expectations, and the report says these are realized values of the given walks."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: a limit-order walk that is rejected once and then fills pays 4 USD in fees and 1000 for 10 shares, with probability 1/32; a loan stream with actual times gives the interest for exactly the time spent; two sessions sum; an unmatched step, an ambiguous step, a time that goes backwards and too many events are rejected"]
  , decision "session-mdp-horizon"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.horizon"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTypes.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "TimeExpr gains period (at next d); schedule places a gated step at (now / d + 1) * d; Text writes it back as at next. Chain edges keep their largest delay and their gate period. Chain.horizon finds the one gate edge g, refuses a cycle among the other edges reachable from the start (depth-first with colours) and a longest path over them, start to g's source or g's target back to it, longer than the period. It solves two absorbing chains with g's source added to the ends, from the start and from g's target, takes q0 and q from their visits at g's source, sums the geometric series exactly with Rat, combines visits as v_init + C v_cycle with C = q0 S(N-1) and sets g's source to the passages q0 S(N); the wait is P x passages minus the joint expected work time to the gate (visits x probability x delay x the probability of reaching the gate from the edge's target), and g's delay becomes the mean wait, so expected, residence, duration and the drawing apply unchanged. session_mdp with horizon parses it as a duration and returns periods, stillRunning, totals and the drawing."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: weekly rent that always continues passes the gate exactly 52 times in 52 weeks, 52000 USD and 52 roomweeks, duration exactly 52 weeks; with a 1/4 chance of notice each week, 4 weeks give 175/64 rents; a loop around the gate, work longer than the period, two gates, a gate without a horizon in the chain and a horizon shorter than a period are rejected; the tree times a gated step at the period boundary"]
  , decision "session-generate-chain"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.generate.chain"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Chain edges keep their delay distribution (after) and, per amount, its outcomes and direction. Chain.generate walks each session from the start: a state with one outgoing edge takes it, a choice draws its branch with Generative.choose over the edges' probabilities, a message or event draws its delay (or waits for the next multiple of its gate period) and each amount's outcome, and emits an Event with prior set to the session's previous event and the drawn amounts beside it; it stops at an end or after 512 events. session_simulate uses it when the protocol needs the chain (hasLoop) and returns events, ended flags and amounts. walkStep reads src/dst as well as from/to and an amounts list; Chain.walk uses the event's amounts in place of the edge's means after checking each against the edge's moves."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: Ride generates 200 sessions with seed 1, every one ended and accepted back by session_mdp events; the realized total equals the sum of each session's drawn amounts and is within 10 percent of 200 times the expected balance; the same seed gives the same stream; an amount that does not match its edge is refused by the walk; a session longer than 512 events is cut and marked not ended"]
  , decision "session-diagram-annotations"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.diagram.annotations"]
      ["LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Diagram.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean"]
      "Annotate.tagStmts appends a control-character tag and the step's index to each message and event label of the resolved body, recording the step's note (branch probability passed to a weighted branch's first step, amounts, time); Text.buildWith builds the tagged body exactly as parse would, so repeated continuations carry their tags; Annotate.strip walks the global type in the order Diagram.draw visits steps, collecting tags and removing them. The chain of the tagged body gives each tag's expected count (traversals of the edges whose step carries it); the legend comes from the chain of the untagged body. Global.diagramModel takes the notes in drawing order and a footer; arrow and act consume the next note and draw it under the step, and the column width allows for the widest note."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: the Ride diagram carries p 4/5, after 30 seconds, 15 USD, 25 USD and x5/4 under the right steps and the per-role legend; no tag reaches the SVG; a protocol without annotations draws the same shapes as the plain model"]
  , decision "session-diagram-folds"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.diagram.folds"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Diagram.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "Diagram.St gains rows (one Diagram.Row per arrow and action box: message id, label, status, sender and receiver as text, the row's top, its arrow's y and its bottom, the branch scope it was drawn in, and the request it answers), scope, a number fresh for every branch of a choice, parallel or join and every body of rec, sessions and include, restored when the body ends, each scope recorded with its parent scope and its frame, and each frame's end (the cursor after its bottom edge). Global.diagramModel keeps the rows as steps and the lifelines as lanes (role and x) with colW, and Diagram.callsOf gives every row that is a request with an id and at least one answering row below it (the reply rule Global.diagram already applies) in its own scope or a scope nested in it a Diagram.Fold: its bottom is the largest of each reply's end, the reply's own bottom in the request's scope, otherwise the end of the frame whose scope is the child of the request's scope on the way up from the reply's scope; with caller, callee, request label, the distinct response labels and statuses in drawing order joined by |, top, y, bottom, depth (one plus the folds whose span contains its span) and the roles of the rows within its span; a reply in a scope not nested in the request's gives nothing. DiagramModel.json writes width, height, shapes, colW, lanes, steps and calls, and every sequence diagram the session tools return goes through it (diagramJson, session_actor's view, session_explore). SessionView.html's diagram() reads calls: a call opens unless its depth is 2 or more; the folded regions are the folded frames (header to foot, nothing kept) and the folded calls (top to bottom, one 30-pixel row kept), taking only regions whose full extent no taken region contains, so the regions are disjoint; y is mapped by subtracting what each region above it removes, shapes inside a region are not drawn, and a folded call is drawn as a line from caller to callee with arrowheads at both ends and the label ▸ Request -> Response status, a hot zone over it opening it; an open call has a ▾ control at its request that folds it. A role is hidden when it has steps and none outside a folded region and it is not the caller or callee of a folded call drawn; when every role with steps would be hidden none is. Hidden lanes are taken out of x with a piecewise map that removes a column per hidden lane (to its right for leading lanes, to its left for trailing ones, centred otherwise), their heads, names and lifelines are not drawn, the width shrinks by colW per lane, and a note hidden: lists them as buttons that open every folded call whose roles name the role and every folded frame holding a step of it. The shapes and calls from LeanFM are never changed; folding state lives only in the view."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SessionTests.lean: checkout's diagram lists its calls with caller, callee, labels, status and depth; a nested call has depth 2 inside its parent's span; Checkout's Order folds over its whole choice with Quote 201 | SoldOut 409, Pay at depth 2 over the nested choice with Receipt 200 | Refused 402, and Verify at depth 3; every fold's span holds its request and every response row and every frame it touches whole; the steps list every message once per drawn arrow, and folding information leaves the shapes as before", "scripts/session_view_smoke.js: a drawn diagram with calls shows a ▸ fold arrow for every call at depth 2 or more; clicking it opens the call and its ▾ control folds it again; a role used only inside folded calls is listed in the hidden note and has no lifeline, and clicking its name brings it back; the view never changes the shapes it was sent"]
  , decision "session-identity-marks"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.identity.marks"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Types.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Grammar.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Syntax.lean", "LeanFM/Session/OpenApi.lean", "LeanFM/Session/LeanGen.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Types.Field gains mark (Mark: plain, fresh, link) and place (Place: body, header, path, query); Field.render writes them back as protocol text (Header (Link String)) and Field.note as a comment for generated code. Grammar.fieldTypeFn reads a type of up to three names, the parentheses of a placement over a mark dropped; Text.structureDecl maps Fresh and Link to marks and Header, PathParam and QueryParam to places, refusing a wrapper inside Fresh or Link and anything but a mark inside a placement's parentheses. Types.check refuses a mark or placement on anything but Nat, Int, String or Bool, and a Link with no Fresh field of its name and type in the text. Syntax declares Fresh, Link, Header, PathParam and QueryParam as abbreviations for the value's type, so a compiled session file elaborates. Types.protoField and leanField append the note; the JSON form writes Field.render. OpenApi makes a placed field a parameter in its place for every method (a path parameter required), and a body-less request's other single fields query parameters as before. SessionTools.logEvent reads fields from the fields, body, headers, path and query objects alike."
      ["LeanFM/Session/IdentityTests.lean: Orders reads orderId as Header (Fresh String) on Created and PathParam (Link String) on GetOrder and writes the types back; the .proto and the Lean module carry plain values with // fresh, header and -- fresh, header; OpenAPI has orderId in the path, required, and verbose in the query; a log with fields in headers, path, query and body conforms, and a header of the wrong type is refused naming the event; a Link with no Fresh of its name, or of its type, a mark on a structure, a mark inside a mark and an Option inside a placement are refused; a Lean structure with Header (Fresh String) compiles and holds the plain value"]
  , decision "session-identity-checks"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.identity.checks", "clause:selfhost.session.log-sessions"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "LogSessions.derive also takes the marked fields (Mark.fresh or Mark.link, by label and field name, from the protocol's types) and a terminated test (SessionTools: Global.next over a session's events so far allows no step). It walks the log in order keeping, per (field, value), the live session that minted it: a Fresh value whose session is live is a re-mint problem; whose session has terminated, or never minted, starts a new session for that value (its event joined to the session its cause names, as any event); a Link value joins its live session, or is a problem when no live session minted it; an event whose Link values name two different live sessions is a join problem and joins neither. Problems are returned with the result; SessionTools.conform adds them to the violations in both modes."
      ["LeanFM/Session/IdentityTests.lean: two orders created and read interleaved without session ids conform as two sessions joined by their orderId Links; an orderId minted again while its order is live is a problem naming both events; after that order's session has ended the same orderId starts a new session; a GetOrder naming an orderId never minted is a problem naming the field and value; one event linking two live orders is a join problem, not a merge"]
  , decision "session-identity-scopes"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.identity.scopes"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Grammar.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Syntax.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Global.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Outline.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Types.lean", "LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Grammar.protocolBody takes an optional given f g after the version; Grammar.incl an optional with b, c = x (Grammar.binding). Text.Def gains givens and Text.Stmt.incl binds (given field, field in scope); Def.write and the statement writer write both back. Text.build refuses an include that binds a field its protocol is not given, binds one twice or leaves one unbound, and names the box Global.boxName (Order with token = sid), which Syntax uses too; Global.boxProtocol recovers the protocol, so Outline and findBox open the box as before. Types.check takes the text's given fields, whose Link needs no Fresh of its own name. Text.scopeCheck walks each protocol in order: a given field marked Fresh inside it is refused; each binding's field must be one the protocol is given or a marked field of a message seen before the include (in its sequence or before the enclosing block; branches see what precedes them), and of the type the included protocol's messages give the given field. Text.scopeCheck.sessionsNote reports a sessions body of two or more messages (deepMsgs, through includes) with no key in which some message is neither a possible first message (firstMsgs), nor names a cause, nor carries a Link to a Fresh field the body mints; Parsed.identityNotes carries the notes, and session_check returns them as identity without changing its verdict. Text.linkTargetsOf walks from the main protocol through includes composing renames, giving each renamed Link (label, field) the field it names; Parsed.linkTargets and Protocol.linkTargets carry it, SessionTools.conform sets LogSessions.Marked.target, and derive looks a Link up by its target. derive frees a session key value whose session has terminated, as it frees a Fresh value: the next event carrying it starts a new session."
      ["LeanFM/Session/IdentityTests.lean: Site (a login minting sid, a sessions body including Order given token with token = sid) checks with no identity note, writes given and with back, draws its box as Order with token = sid, and Global.boxProtocol gives Order; an unbound, unknown, twice-bound, out-of-scope (before the login) or mistyped binding, and a given field marked Fresh inside Order, are refused naming the field; a sessions body whose Return names no cause is noted with verdict ok, while a cause, a key or a single message leaves no note, and a Get carrying only the given token is noted; a log of one login with two orders places every event with no identity problem, two interleaved logins derive two sessions, a task message carrying the other login's token is a join problem, and a token no login minted is a Link problem; a caller whose keyed session ended starts a new session with the same key; a compiled session file with given and with elaborates and its box is TaskP with token"]
  , decision "session-identity-children"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.identity.children"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Text.linkTargetsOf lists every Link field a message carries because an include binds it, renamed or under its own name (token to sid, or token to token), so Parsed.linkTargets names the given Links; it counts the sessions blocks entered on the way (through includes) and records each binding with the count where its value was minted (a field the includer mints, at the include's count; a field the includer is given, at its own binding's), so Parsed.parentLinks lists the given Links whose message lies inside more sessions blocks than its value's minting. SessionTools.conform marks those LogSessions.Marked.parent; the other given Links stay ordinary Links that join. LogSessions.derive splits an event's Link values: a parent Link is checked live exactly as before (a problem when no live session minted the value) and is recorded as (position, root of the minting session, field, value) without a union; the other Links join as before. After the union-find, each group's parents are the roots its parent Links named, resolved to their final roots: one parent becomes Session.parent (the parent session's name, added to Result.json as parent), two or more are a problem naming the event, field, value and both sessions, the event staying in its group. A parent Link marks its event as grown, so a child's first message (Create) is a session though no session starts with it. Global.allNestedSessions lists the sessions blocks nested at any depth; Global.conforms falls back to each of them in turn, and SessionTools.conform's terminated asks Global.next of the body and then of each nested block, so a child's minted values are freed when its own block can take no further step. Logged mode (crossCheck) and protocols with no given fields are unchanged apart from that freeing."
      ["LeanFM/Session/IdentityTests.lean: one login running two orders then logging out conforms as three sessions, the login with Login, LoggedIn, Logout and Bye and each order a child naming it as parent; two logins each running an order conform as four sessions with the right parents; an order message carrying another login's token is a problem naming both sessions and stays in its order's session; a token no login minted is still a problem; an order's orderId is freed when the order ends, so a second order minting the same orderId under the same login conforms; with token (same name) derives children as with token = sid does; a protocol without given fields derives the same sessions as before"]
  , decision "session-identity-joins"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.identity.joins"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html", "LeanFM/SessionView.lean"]
      "A field in scope for with is any field of an earlier message (Text.scopeCheck no longer requires a mark), so a plain field read from a listing is a reference that may be bound. LogSessions.derive keeps each parent Link as (position, minting position, field, value); after the union-find each session's references are grouped by field: one field naming two different parent sessions stays a problem naming the event, field, value and both sessions; different fields naming different sessions are a join, accepted because only given fields that the include binds are parent Links. Session.parents lists the parents in order of their field's first reference and Session.parent is the first; Result.json writes parent and, when there are two or more, parents. A Fresh value minted by a session's own events names that session, so a protocol included in a sessions body that mints an id (GameCreated) is a session of its own and later copies given that id join it. SessionView.html's streamView passes each derived session's parents with its frame; animation nests a frame under its first visible parent and writes the tab as session key, joins and the parents' keys."
      ["LeanFM/Session/IdentityTests.lean: a person creates a game in a sessions copy (the game is a child of the person's session); another person picks the game's id from a listing (a plain field, bound by with) and plays in a sessions copy given both ids: the play session is a join whose parents are the player's session and the game, the listing stays in the player's session, and the log conforms; a play copy whose gameId names a second game is a problem naming both; the crossed-token problem (one field, two sessions) is unchanged; binding a plain field of an earlier message is accepted and binding a field no earlier message carries is refused"]
  , decision "session-faithful"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.faithful"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Faithful.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Session/Faithful.lean: LGraph is a checker model (Logic.buildModel over the protocol's Global.draft, or over a draft given) as node labels (last message, may stop) and successor arrays; refine is signature refinement: round 0 numbers the labels, each later round numbers the pairs (class, sorted distinct successor classes) through a hash map, stopping when no class splits, so a round is one pass over the steps and the rounds are the lookahead plus one. typestates keeps the rounds, a representative per class and each class's next classes; deterministic holds when no class has two next classes with the same label. walk pairs the candidate's start with the protocol's start class and walks breadth first, checking at each candidate node that its label is its class's and that its next labels are exactly the class's, and giving each successor the one next class with its label; a successor already paired with another class is unfaithful (the candidate merges what the protocol keeps apart); it is O(m). byRefinement refines the union of both graphs and, when the starts differ, searches breadth first through pairs with equal labels that the refinement keeps apart for the first pair whose labels or next labels differ, returning the path's messages and both sides' next labels; compare uses walk when the protocol's typestates are deterministic and byRefinement otherwise. names builds one formula per class from the rounds, each round's from the round before so they are shared in memory (label: after M or no message yet, and terminal or not; then EX of each next class and AX of their or); rules writes the start and AG (name -> EX each next and AX their or) per class. SessionTools.faithful answers session_ctl's faithful argument: rules lists at most 64 rules, each as an s-expression from Logic.Dag.toSexpr with its subformula count and, when small, its notation; task or lean builds the candidate's model with Session.sessionBounds and answers faithful or unfaithful with the method, path, next labels and a reading. MCP.lean declares faithful in session_ctl's schema; the session reference describes it."
      ["LeanFM/Session/FaithfulTests.lean: LeanFM's own protocol has 35 typestates, lookahead 1 and 36 rules; every rule is proved by session_ctl's checker over the protocol; over more than 100 broken copies of its draft (each step dropped, steps retargeted, relabelled and added) the walk says faithful exactly when the CTL checker proves every rule, more than 90 are caught, and walk and refinement agree on every copy; the protocol is faithful to itself by the walk, by draft and by text; a dropped step is unfaithful with a reading naming what the protocol allows there; a draft whose two branches both start with a is not deterministic, is faithful to itself by refinement, and is not faithful to a then (b or c), either way round"]
  , decision "session-ctl-scale"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.ctl-scale"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/Session/SpecCheck.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Logic.lean: Dag hash-conses a formula: DagKey (operator name, children's node numbers, the atom's repr) indexes a Std.HashMap whose BEq compares the whole key, so a hash collision costs a comparison; Dag.add walks the formula with a memo keyed by the address of each part (ptrAddrUnsafe, as @[implemented_by] of addRef, which walks every path and gives the same DAG because equal addresses are equal values), so parts shared in memory are walked once. Formula.atoms, the size bounds and the answers use the DAG: treeSize counts the tree up to a cap, depth the nesting, toSexpr writes the formula with each part of two or more parents labelled. parseFormula, parseBounded and the new parseShared take the caller's Bounds; parseBounded (explain_formula, classify_requirement, check_protocol, check_reassembly) bounds the tree, parseShared (check_ctl) the distinct subformulas; SpecCheck.sessionBounds allows 65,536. Core.ofDag rewrites the DAG into COp (tt, ff, atom, not, and, or, ex, ax, eu, au, eg) with satTree's meanings (EF as EU true, AF as AU true, AG as not EU true not, never, implies, stronglyImplies, EW and AW as satTree writes them), hash-consed again. Model.satE evaluates the core DAG in node order with ByteArray bitsets, the steps as Csr offset and target arrays both ways; EX and AX are one pass over the steps; EU a backward worklist from the b-nodes through a-nodes; AU a worklist where a node joins when its count of steps not yet satisfied reaches zero and a holds; EG removes, by worklist, every a-node left with no successor inside; atoms are satTree's. Each node's readers are counted and a set is freed when they are done; the bytes held are summed and passing Bounds.maxTruthBytes (1 GiB) stops with an error that nothing was proved. Model.sat is satE (no node past the bound); checkCtl calls satE, answers resource-limit past the bound, and writes formula, notation and reading as the s-expression when the formula's tree would pass treeLimit (4,096) nodes. The old fixpoint evaluation stays as satTree, the reference the tests compare with."
      ["LeanFM/Session/CtlScaleTests.lean: over a draft with a loop, a choice, an ending and a deadlock and over every canonical task, satE equals satTree at every node for every formula of depth two over four atoms and every operator; a formula of about 2^26 tree nodes and 76 DAG nodes is evaluated and checked by check_ctl, whose answer writes it as a labelled s-expression; parseShared refuses it at 75 distinct subformulas and accepts it at 76; parseBounded applies a bound of 2 it is given; parseFormula applies a depth bound of 2 it is given; satE stops past a truth-set bound of 2 bytes saying nothing was proved, and check_ctl answers resource-limit; equal subformulas are one node and different ones two"]
  , decision "session-ctl-sexpr"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.ctl-sexpr"]
      ["LeanFM/Logic.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Logic.lean: sexprTokens reads ( ) names (a run of letters, digits and _ . : / @ $ -, or a double-quoted string with backslash escapes), #n= and #n#; any other # form or character is refused. sexprFormula reads one formula with the caller's Bounds (depth checked at each nesting): a #n= label is recorded only after its expression is read, so a reference to it inside its own expression, or before it, is refused, as is a label defined twice; operators take the arities of the JSON form, after, at and claimed one name, secret from, to and field pairs, K, O, P, B, Pr and C are unsupported as in JSON, and knows, field, predicate and derived are refused naming the JSON form. A reference returns the formula already read, so labelled parts are shared in memory and Dag.add walks them once. parseSexpr refuses text past maxArgumentBytes or after the formula; parseFormula reads a JSON string beginning with ( or # through it, so every tool that reads a formula accepts it. The logic reference and the session reference describe the form, and MCP.lean's check_ctl and session_ctl schemas mention it."
      ["LeanFM/Session/CtlScaleTests.lean: s-expressions read as the same formulas as their JSON forms (implies, until, secret, the constants, at and quoted claimed names, an unsupported K); a labelled part reads as the part written out; an undefined label, a label defined twice, a self-referring label, a #. form, a bracket, knows, after without a name, text after the formula and a depth past the bound given are refused; distinct subformulas count a repeated part once; a formula written back by toSexpr reads as itself; check_ctl gives the same verdict and path for an s-expression and its JSON form"]
  , decision "session-log-sessions"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.log-sessions"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Session/LogSessions.lean: derive takes the parsed log (LogEvent), the protocol's declared session keys (Protocol.keys, (message id, field path)) with the labels each message id can carry, and the labels a session can start with (the steps from the first state of Global.stateGraph); it runs a union-find over the events: a cause joins an event to the event it names, and a key joins events whose label carries that key's message id and that log the same value at its field; prev and where-clause equations are never read. A group no join made larger than one event is a session if its event's label can start one, and is otherwise unattributed. Sessions are numbered in order of their first event and named by their key values (caller=alice) or, without one, by chain from the first event's id; at most 200 are listed and the rest counted. crossCheck lists, for a log that names sessions, every cause or key join whose two events name different log sessions, as a problem naming both events, their sessions and the link. SessionTools.conform reads whether each raw event has a session field: none is derived mode, where each event's session becomes its derived session's name before the replay and each unattributed event is a problem (the verdict violates); every one is logged mode, where the cross-check's problems join the violations; a mix is refused. Every answer carries derivedSessions (sessions with key, events as positions, logSessions; unattributed as positions) and events: the log in session_simulate's shape (id numbered from 1 in log order, src, dst, label, act, cause and prior as those numbers, session), so SessionView.html's streamView draws a log with its session buttons and focus unchanged."
      ["LeanFM/Session/LogSessionsTests.lean: msg, msg, resp, resp from two sessions without ids conform when each resp names its msg as cause, in any interleaving; without causes the two resps are unattributed, never guessed; two orders interleaved without session ids are told apart by their cause chains and both conform; the same log with session ids conforms and its derived sessions agree; a log whose session ids split a cause chain is a problem naming both events and the cause; two sessions keyed by caller are joined by the key value though no cause links them; two payments with equal amounts in different sessions are not joined (a where equation is no identity); one server instance B/b1 answering both sessions, its second resp naming its first as prev, does not join them (an actor named by its role alone is that role in its own session, so a shared server is named as an instance); a log naming a session on some events only is refused; every answer carries derivedSessions and the log as a stream numbered from 1"]
  , decision "session-derived-sessions"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.derived-sessions"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Derived.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "LeanFM/Session/Derived.lean: equations reads every message's where clause with Where.parse and keeps each comparison x.f = y.g between two paths found at the top of the condition or under and (never under or, not or another comparison), at most 64; group runs a union-find with path compression over the events (at most 600, in stream order): for each equation class (the (message id, field) pairs joined by equations, closed transitively) every event whose message id and field are in the class is joined with the first event holding the same JSON value in that class, recording the label field=value on the event; cause and prior links join an event to the events they name. Each root's events, oldest first, form a session with its key (the distinct labels of its events joined by ', ', or chain from e<id> of its first event when it has none), count, event indices, the distinct simulated session numbers (a cross-check), and joinedBy naming the first event that carries two different labels when equations over different fields joined groups; events with no label and no link are unattributed. The answer lists at most 200 sessions by size, then first event, and counts the rest, and counts the sessions matching exactly one simulated session. SessionTools.simulate adds derivedSessions to all three of its answers (structural, timed and chain), from the events it returns with their fields flattened by flattenFields. SessionView.html's streamView draws a bar with all, one button per derived session (key and count) and unattributed, the session buttons inside a div of class sesslist (role group, 120 px tall at most, overflow-y auto) so they scroll while all and unattributed stay outside it, and picking one rebuilds the animation with sessionBody over exactly those indices and roles reduced to those with an event left, a hidden: note listing the rest; the simulated session number stays a label in the log; nothing the tools sent is changed."
      ["LeanFM/Session/DerivedTests.lean: two rooms' events split by roomId equations into roomId=r1 and roomId=r2; a response with no field joins its request through cause; an event no equation or link reaches is unattributed; an equation under or is not a join; equations over roomId and userId sharing an event merge into one session whose key names both and whose joinedBy names that event; no event is in two sessions; the simulated session numbers are reported and counted as matching", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean and scripts/session_view_smoke.js: session_simulate for the lending example answers derivedSessions; the stream shows a button per derived session, picking one shows only its events in the log and lists roles with none left as hidden"]
  , decision "session-rec-bounded"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.rec.bounded"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Text.lean"]
      "The loop parser reads at most N and an optional otherwise block into Stmt.bounded. Text.unroll, applied to every protocol body as soon as the file is parsed, replaces it by copy 1, where each continue Name (in any nested branch) becomes copy k+1, and at copy N the otherwise block; copy k renames the body's declared ids (and causes and time references to them) with a _k suffix. Nothing after parsing sees Stmt.bounded."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: Ride with Dispatch at most 3 has no loop left, runs in the tree view, gives P(no driver) = 1/125 and 31/25 expected offers by hand, and its expected balances equal the unbounded ride's times the probability of a driver; renamed ids keep their causes; a bound of 0 or 33 is refused"]
  , decision "session-mdp-distribution"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.distribution"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Decision.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Decision.distribution groups terminal masses by each role and unit's balance. Chain.periodPaths enumerates, from a state, every path over the edges other than the gate until the gate's source or an end, branching on choices, on each delay outcome and on each amount outcome, carrying the path's probability, work time and rewards (trade vectors and rates in force times the drawn delay). Chain.horizonDistribution starts from the start's paths and, for each period, adds the gate's own amounts and the rates in force at the gate times the wait (the period minus the work time), then extends every running outcome by the cycle's paths, keeping outcomes in a hash map keyed by their reward vector, with exact Rat masses. Marginals per role and unit and the passages distribution (from q0 and q) are read off at the end. Histograms are drawn as bars of probability over sorted values."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: the bounded ride's Rider pays 25, 15, 5 or 0 USD with 279/625, 279/625, 62/625 and 1/125; rent with repairs over 4 weeks has a distribution whose mean equals the expected 742025/216 USD and whose passages distribution matches q0 q^k; probabilities sum to 1; a chain with loops and no horizon gives no distribution and says why; too many outcomes is refused"]
  , decision "session-mdp-optimize"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.mdp.optimize"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Chain.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "Chain.build with open choices allowed turns an unweighted choice into an open state whose policy edges carry probability zero and the branch's first message; Chain.analyse skips the probability check and the solve for open states. Chain.optimize computes, backwards from the ends, the states that can reach an end with some choice (an open state takes the first branch that can), which is the initial policy; it then evaluates the policy by solving (I - Q) V = R exactly (R per state is the sum of probability times the edge's reward for the objective: the role's signed share of the trade vector and of the rates in force times the delay, or the delay for time), and for each open state switches to the strictly best branch r(e) + V(dst) when the switched policy still lets every state reach an end, until no switch helps or 100 rounds. The chosen branches get probability 1 and the chain is solved again for the full answer."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ChainTests.lean: Dispatch with an open standard-or-boost choice: the Platform's best is standard with 27/4 USD against 152/39 for boost, the Driver's best is boost with 266/13 USD; the values match the hand-solved equations; a protocol where the open choice can only loop is refused; a policy that never ends is never chosen; without optimize the open choice is refused"]
  , decision "session-sla"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.sla"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/GenerativeTypes.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Sla.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "Generative.Requirement holds the entry id, the bound (every, atLeast p, atMost p), the targets, within, window (time or entries), confidence, expected rate and the clause text. Text.fileDefs reads require per q { ... } (the grammar's requireBody) after the protocols into Acc.requires; Text.parse checks that q is a declared message id of the main protocol and every target a label of some message or event. Sla.design builds the chain view (Chain.analyse on the resolved generative layer), takes as entries the message edges whose label is q's, and from each entry runs a breadth-first enumeration over (state, elapsed ms) with exact masses merged per pair: an edge to a target within the deadline adds to hits; an edge past the deadline, an entry edge, or an end adds to misses; at most 20000 expansions, the rest unresolved. The verdict compares the interval [hits, hits + unresolved] with the bound for the worst entry. The most probable miss (or hit, for at most) path is kept with its labels. Sla.windowNeed is ceil(ln(1/(1 - c)) / margin) with margin 1 - p for at least and p for at most."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SlaTests.lean: UserLookup's 504 timeout makes every q reach a response within 2 seconds met; its 1/100 database failures against at most 1/200 of 503s is violated with exact probability 1/100 and the failing path through the lookup choice; the latency bound is met with an exact interval; the window needs 300 entries at 99/100 and 95/100 confidence; refusals for a missing window, a window on every, an unknown id or label, and a choice without probabilities"]
  , decision "session-execution"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.execution"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Sla.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "Sla.Execution holds the events in a Std.HashMap keyed by timestamp, the timestamps in order, a forward index (children by cause and prev) and the sparse reward map. causalPast walks prev and cause backwards; view is the union of the causal pasts of the role's own events; rewardAt walks prev backwards from the event to the first timestamp in the reward map and returns that vector (the zero vector when the chain starts without one); Execution.make refuses a reward vector equal to the one it would inherit through prev. observe takes each entry, walks its causal future forwards (only events up to the deadline), and records hit, miss or pending (deadline after the last timestamp). Windows group entries by floor(ts / W) or in consecutive blocks of N; a window is closed when the last timestamp is at least its end plus the deadline (time) or it holds N decided entries, and only a closed window is judged. The binomial tails are summed in log space in Float (n up to 20000) and compared with 1 - confidence."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SlaTests.lean: duplicate timestamps, a cause to a later or missing event are refused; the reward at an event is the vector at the nearest event back along prev that has one (a concurrent event's vector is not taken), and an unchanged vector is refused; a role's view holds its events and their causes; 300 entries all answered within 300 ms meet at least 99/100 at 95/100 confidence, 290 of 300 violate it, 299 of 300 are undecided; an entry whose deadline outlives the stream is pending; a single miss in a window whose period has not closed is open, not violated"]
  , decision "session-events"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.events"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Events.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean"]
      "SessionTools.simFields fills each simulated message's fields from its message type (a Fresh field a new value at each minting event, sessionId sess-n for session n and any other field name-k for event k, remembered by name; a Link field the value last minted for its target, Protocol.linkTargets naming the field it stands for; unmarked: sessionId from simSessionId, requestId from a counter, other scalars small examples, nested messages Ty.example) and then makes its where clause true with fillWhere over the parsed clause (equalities copy from the latest event of the named message id in the session or set a literal, and both sides of and, the first workable side of or); any other clause gives the stream no fields. Global.simulate runs Sim.run in StateM over threads (session, remaining protocol, loop environment, join, start step, the fuel each loop gets, the repetitions left to each loop it is in, last event, events to follow), joins (need, done, fired, continuation, the loops' remaining repetitions, the ends of finished branches) and the events. Sim.rand is a 64-bit linear congruential generator. Each round takes the threads whose start has come (or moves the clock to the earliest start), removes one at random and steps it: choice picks a random branch, among only the branches that leave every loop whose fuel is spent (Sim.leaves) when there are any, box becomes andThen, loop pushes its environment and gives the loop its fuel, var unrolls the loop's body while that loop's fuel lasts and otherwise ends, parallel and join create a join and one thread per branch, sessions creates a join and the requested number of instance threads with random start delays up to spread, msg and act emit one event and requeue the thread. emit computes prior from the last event of each involved role in the session plus the thread's pending join ends, cause from the session's message ids, and answers by walking cause events to the first request from the receiver to the sender. A finished thread passes its last event to its join, which starts its continuation once need branches have finished. Sessions are renumbered by first event. eventsJson writes one JSON object per event; jsonString escapes quotes, backslashes, newlines and control characters."
      ["LeanFM/Session/SessionTests.lean: five Serve sessions give ten events, each session Get then Page, Page answering Get; for twenty seeds Checkout's stream links only backwards and holds exactly one run's messages; the same seed gives the same JSON; Fan's parallel branches on different actors are not linked through prior and Done follows both; Transfer with fuel 2 sends at most three Data and, for twelve seeds, always ends with LastAck; two loops in a row with fuel 1 always reach EndB with at most one B; LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: GoServer finishes its game for eight seeds and the bot plays in at least one; its messages carry sessionId sess-1 and fields, and three seeds' streams, fields included, conform; a clause t.n > q.n leaves the stream without fields; jsonString escapes a quote, a backslash and a newline"]
  , decision "session-where"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.where", "clause:selfhost.session.conform"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Where.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Skeleton.lean", "LeanFM/Session/SkeletonTs.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Diagram.lean"]
      "LeanFM.Session.Where parses a condition's text (Guard.text, the same in the text form and the Lean form) into a closed Expr by recursive descent (or, and, not, comparison, sum, product, unary minus, atoms) and evaluates it with an interpreter over Value (num Rat, str, bool): there is no constructor holding code. Where.eval takes a lookup from a message id to its event's fields (dotted keys) and answers .yes, .no or .unknown with a reason; comparison of different types, a missing field, an unbound id and division by zero are .unknown. Where.paths lists (id, field path) pairs a condition reads. LogEvent gains fields, parsed by SessionTools.logEvent from the event's fields object, nested objects flattened to dotted keys, numbers as exact rationals, strings and booleans kept, others dropped; at most 64 fields, keys and strings within 200 characters. Conform.step evaluates the guard of a matching message with the session's bindings plus the current event: .no drops that transition, .unknown keeps it and records an unchecked entry carried in the state (outside its key); checkSession reports, when no state survives an event and some transition was dropped only by its guard, the condition and the values it read, and returns the unchecked entries of a surviving state. session_conform adds unchecked per session and their count to the reading. Text.parse refuses a condition Where cannot parse or Types cannot type (decision session-types). Skeleton and SkeletonTs give each send the list of fields conditions read for its message id (from Where.paths over every guard) and log fields: the body projected to exactly those paths. Diagram.draw, for a choice, takes the guard of each branch's first message as that operand's guard: under the frame tab for the first operand, on the divider for the others, and draws that message without it."
      ["LeanFM/Session/WhereTests.lean: parse and evaluate every operator; precedence; exact rational arithmetic; a missing field, an unbound id, a type mismatch and division by zero are unknown with their reason; text outside the language is unknown; PlaceOrder with p.amount = q.price conforms with matching fields, is violated with 12 against 10 and the values named, and reports unchecked without fields; a guard selects between branches by data; logEvent flattens nested fields and refuses 65 fields; an unparseable condition and an unknown id are refused when the text is read; the generated Python and TypeScript log only the fields conditions read (amount and price, not card); the diagram draws each branch's condition as its operand guard, once"]
