      ["clause:selfhost.session.actor-conformance", "clause:selfhost.session.timelines", "clause:selfhost.session.critical-path"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Actors.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Spans.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean: LogEvent gains srcActor and dstActor (the logged Role/instance, empty when only the role is named), prev, sentAt and receivedAt (nanoseconds on the sender's and the receiver's clock). LeanFM/SessionTools.lean: logEvent splits from and to with Actors.parse (Role or Role/instance, refusing empty parts and more than one /), keeps the role for the protocol and the actor name, reads sentAt and receivedAt as decimal strings or safe JSON integers with wireNat (null is absent) and prev as text. LeanFM/Session/Actors.lean: src and dst give an event's actors (the logged name, or Role@session); reading is an actor's clock reading at an event (sentAt for its sends, receivedAt for its receives); clockOrder links each event to the latest earlier reading of each of its actors' clocks; links are prev, cause and those clock links; check reports duplicate ids, a prev naming no event or an event of another sender or itself, a cause whose event does not involve the sender, a fork in an actor's prev chain, and a cycle (Kahn's algorithm over links leaves events unplaced); linearize places events in log order whenever all their links within the list are placed. conform reports check's problems with the session problems and replays Global.conforms over linearize of the log, so each session sees its events in happens-before order. LeanFM/Session/Spans.lean: spans pairs each request with the first reply naming it as cause from its receiver back to its sender, callerView = reply.receivedAt - request.sentAt and calleeView = reply.sentAt - request.receivedAt (absent when a reading is missing or the difference is negative); before follows prev and cause back from an event; subcalls of a span are spans whose caller is its callee, sent on the callee's clock between receiving the request and replying, and after the request in happens-before; awaited means the sub-call's reply was received before the parent's reply was sent (callee's clock); heaviestChain is weighted interval scheduling over awaited sub-calls ordered by reply receipt, a chain's next sub-call sent at or after the previous reply came back; report gives the network time (callerView - calleeView), the critical path (heaviest chain by caller views), the bound (heaviest chain by own durations; with every sub-call contained in the span it holds whenever no callee exceeds its caller's wait, and is checked anyway), and issues for a callee longer than its caller's wait and for a bound above the span; place lays a tree on its root's timeline with offset(callee) = offset(caller) + request.sentAt - request.receivedAt; roots are spans that are no span's sub-call. session_spans returns spans, timelines (root, events with atNs) and issues, verdict measured or inconsistent, and a reading naming each root's caller wait and critical path; it is registered in MCP and in SessionTools.run."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ActorTests.lean: a lookup through an api to a database and a cache in parallel, four clocks off by +5 s, -3 s and +1 hour: the log conforms in its order, reversed, replies first, and shuffled, and a wrong reply violates in every order; a prev from another actor, a cause the sender never saw, a forked own order, a prev cycle, a reply received before its request on one clock, a prev naming nothing and an actor with two slashes are reported; the browser waited 18 ms, the api took 14 ms, 4 ms were network; the database took 10 ms against the api's 12 ms wait, the cache 2 ms against 4 ms; the query and the cache overlapped, so the critical path is the query alone at 12 ms; on the browser's timeline the api answers at 14 ms, the browser has it at 18 ms and the database's reply is at 11 ms; a database claiming 13 ms against a 12 ms wait is inconsistent with both numbers; a reply after the parent's reply is listed not awaited; without readings no duration is given", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: session_spans is listed among the tools"]
  , decision "session-versions"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.versions"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Versions.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Grammar.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Text.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Syntax.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Types.lean", "LeanFM/Session/LeanGen.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Conform.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean"]
      "Grammar.protocolBody takes an optional version N after the roles. Text.protocolDef reads it into Def.version (1 to Text.maxVersion = 9999; 0 is no version). fileDefs allows a name again only when both are versioned and the new version is higher than the newest so far, and refuses an unversioned repeat or a versioned protocol mixed with an unversioned one; Text.latest is the newest definition of a name, used by include (build) and accept (systemDecl). Text.parse builds every version of every versioned protocol against the protocols before it into Parsed.versioned and records Parsed.version. Versions.search finds a clean upgrade path: it projects both versions, and breadth first over sets of upgraded shared roles (at most maxShared = 8) explores each mixed deployment, the old version's roles with the upgraded ones on their new local types, with Evolution.explore, the composed-system search session_compat uses; the start and the end are the two protocols, so roles only in the new version join with the last upgrade and roles only in the old one leave with it; a set is explored once, the first full order is returned, and when none exists the deepest stuck mixtures are returned (Stuck.text: the upgraded roles, the path and what each role expects); versions sharing no role have no path. Versions.check runs it on each consecutive pair and refuses with Versions.refusal; SessionTools.parseProtocol calls it, so every tool refuses such a text, and carries version, versions, upgrades and Versions.labelHistory (the main protocol's labels in order of first appearance across its versions). Syntax.elabProtocol defines an unversioned protocol as before and version N as Name.vN: it refuses a version after an unversioned definition or not higher than the newest (newestVersion looks Name.vK up in the namespace and by resolution), then defines the candidate in withoutModifyingEnv, evaluates it and the version before with evalProtocol (Term.evalTerm of the constant applied to its role names, implemented_by an unsafe function) and runs Versions.search; only a version with a clean path is defined, so a refused one cannot be included. Includes of a versioned protocol are retargeted to its newest Name.vK before generation (includesOf, retarget; gen names the constant with String.toName and shows the plain name in the box). Types.proto puts protocol = 11 and version = 12 in the envelope header (13 to 15 kept), payloads from firstPayloadField = 16 numbered by their place in the history, so a label keeps its number in every version, and a label the newest version dropped is written as reserved by number and snake name. Global.leanModule takes the version and the declared versions and writes protocolName, protocolVersion, acceptedVersions (its own and the newest one below it, as Versions.accepted), negotiate (the highest common version, none when there is none), Event.protocol and Event.version defaulting to them, and admits. LogEvent gains protocolName and version, read by logEvent (0 is no version); versionProblems refuses, for a versioned protocol, an event naming no version, a version the text does not keep, or another protocol, and a session naming two versions, and for an unversioned one an event naming a version; conform replays each version's events against that version's global type and reads version problems first. session_compat without old compares the newest two versions of the text and always answers upgradePath (clean with the order, or the stuck mixtures)."
      ["LeanFM/Session/VersionTests.lean: Lookup versions 1, 2 and 4 are Lean commands defining Lookup.v1, Lookup.v2 and Lookup.v4; version 3, which renames the request, is a compile error naming both first steps and where they get stuck (#guard_msgs), and is not defined; a repeated version 2 and a version after an unversioned Plain are compile errors; Front's plain include takes version 4 (labels Ask, Get, Missing); the tools find Client then Server for 1 to 2 and Server then Client for 2 to 4, refuse the version 3 text with the same reason, and refuse versions out of order, repeated, mixed with an unversioned one, and version 0; versions sharing no role have no path; lending v1 to v2 has the clean order Desk, Member, Librarian although the Member cannot go first; session_compat over the text's versions reads the order and answers upgradePath; the proto has protocol = 11 and version = 12, Get 16 and Found 17 in every version, Missing 18 from version 2, and in version 4 Found's 17 reserved with its name while Missing keeps 18; the Lean module of version 4 has protocolVersion 4, acceptedVersions [2, 4] and the envelope fields; negotiate picks 2 or 4 and none without a common version; a log with a version 1 and a version 2 session conforms, Missing in a version 1 session violates, and an event naming no version, version 7, two versions in a session and another protocol are each reported", "LeanFM/Session/EnvelopeTests.lean: payloads at 16, 17, 18 and the protocol and version header fields", "LeanFM/SessionToolsTests.lean: session_compat readings name the clean upgrade path (lending: Desk then Member then Librarian)"]
  , decision "session-layout"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.layout"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/Layout.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html", "LeanFM/SessionView.lean"]
      "LeanFM/Session/Layout.lean: backEdges runs an iterative depth-first search from the start, then from each state not yet reached, marking an edge to a state on the stack; columns gives each state its longest forward path by Kahn's order over the other edges; layout adds a point per column crossed by a long edge (a back edge's chain reversed), orders each column breadth first from the start, then runs 12 barycentre sweeps (down by predecessors, up by successors, ties by position) keeping an order only when crossings (counted per pair of adjacent columns) fall, sizes each box by its label (7 px a character plus padding, 28 px high), places columns left to right each as wide as its widest box, and places rows by 24 alternating sweeps of placeRows: the least-squares rows nearest the neighbours' mean under minimum gaps (16 px between boxes, 8 beside a point), by pooling adjacent violators over the gap-shifted values; routes run from a source's right side through its points to the target's left side (a back edge from the left side to the right). stateLabel names a state start, end, by its one or two message or event labels (send L to R as L → R, receive L from R as L ← R), or by its own name. machineJson answers width, height, nodes (id, label, centre, size, ends) and edges (points, back, self) in the order of the answer's edges, or nothing over 400 states or when an edge names an unknown state. SessionTools.explore with states adds layout. SessionView.html's graphView, given a layout, draws it as given: rounded boxes (a double border where the session may stop, the start outlined amber, the current one filled), messages solid (received ones cyan), structural steps dashed and named, a back or long edge smoothed through its points, the arrowhead along the last segment; the drawing fits the visible width (zoom down to 0.02, never above 1), again on every resize until the user zooms, pans or picks a zoom key; wheel and keys zoom from 0.02 to 4 and the canvas is as tall as it needs (140 to 640 px); a click inside a box picks it, the token runs along the route, and moves name their target by its label; without a layout it keeps the circle layout (the grammar walk, which grows as it goes)."
      ["LeanFM/Session/LayoutTests.lean: a machine with a loop, a choice and a long edge: no boxes overlap, all inside the drawing, forward edges run left to right and the one back edge right to left, the long edge passes three points, the same input gives the same layout; two chains given crossed are uncrossed; placeRows keeps its gaps and leaves a placement that already keeps them; names: Initialize ← Host, two names joined, three or a structural step give the state's own name, start and end; negative: 401 states, an unknown start or an edge to an unknown state get no layout; LeanFM's own Server: 21 boxes, Initialize ← Host and end with a double border", "scripts/session_view_smoke.js: session_explore states carries a layout; the view draws it, says laid out by LeanFM and names the current state start; taking a transition moves it"]
  , decision "session-explorer"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.actor", "clause:selfhost.session.dataset"]
      ["LeanFM/Session/ActorView.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Dataset.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Types.lean", "LeanFM/Session/Generative.lean", "LeanFM/SessionTools.lean", "LeanFM/MCP.lean", "LeanFM/SessionView.html"]
      "ActorView.lean holds Global.silent (no message and no event anywhere) and Global.restrict r, a structural recursion keeping msg when r is its sender or receiver and act when it is r's, rebuilding choice (unless every branch is silent), parallel and join (dropping silent branches, keeping the continuation), loop, sessions and box (dropped when their body is silent); lanes filters the protocol's roles to r and the senders and receivers of the restricted messages; rows lists Global.messages involving r and r's own events once each, with direction, peer, route, status and the field number from Types.payloadNumbers, which Types.proto now also uses for the oneof, so the two cannot disagree. SessionTools.actor validates the role against the roles, then answers the projection (Global.project: local type and typestate, or its error), the restricted diagram model with the role's lanes and its SVG, rows, calls (routed requests r sends) and serves, Global.openApi as JSON and as yaml, the whole protocol's proto, and provenance. Dataset.lean bounds a request (maxSessions 10000, maxEvents 200000, maxSessionEvents 600, 12 paths, 32 rows, 12 histogram ranges). walk starts from the body of a top-level sessions block with nested sessions inlined (as Global.next does) and at each step takes Conform.nextOf of every current state, plus the end when some state is nullable, picks one with Sim's LCG (high bits), and advances every state with Conform.step on a LogEvent carrying the step's cause, deduplicating by stateKey; no option is stuck, the end ends it, and reaching the cap marks it truncated, not ended. walkMany seeds session i with sessionSeed seed i (SplitMix64) and refuses passing maxEvents. summary counts paths (labels joined by ->, 16 written out, cut or stuck marked), messages by label, sender and receiver, the sessions containing each, sent and received per role (load), and lengths and durations into histograms, with decimal2 means. SessionTools.dataset refuses a protocol with issues, then generates by the layer: with a loop, Chain.generate one session at a time at sessionSeed with the per-session cap (ended from its ended flag, otherwise truncated) and the total checked; without one, Generative.generate with the total as its limit; with no layer, Dataset.walkMany; it merges the summary with the reading (counts, distinct paths, the most common path and its share, the sessions that did not end, and whether the frequencies estimate stated probabilities or describe the walker), basis and provenance. Generative.choose builds its ticket from the high 32 bits of each LCG state: taken from the whole state modulo an even denominator it followed the parity of the draws before it (Ride's 1/2 trip length came out 56/44), which also corrected session_simulate and session_mdp walks; Generative.generate collects its reports in an Array. The view's navigation calls session_actor, session_dataset and session_types and draws their answers (actorPanels, datasetPanels, codeBox, bars)."
      ["LeanFM/Session/ExploreTests.lean: the restricted Shop keeps exactly each role's messages (Bank: Charge, Paid, Declined), every restricted message involves the role, a choice Bank sees on one branch keeps an empty branch, lanes are Shop and Bank for Bank; rows give direction, peer and field 17, 18, 20 as the proto file numbers them; Shop serves POST /orders and calls POST /charges, and its YAML holds /orders only; the reading is pinned; an unknown role and a missing role are refused; 300 walks all end and every one conforms by Global.conforms; one seed gives one dataset and two seeds two; 2000 sessions give 3 paths, all ended, basis walk and the walker sentence; a loop that never ends is cut at 600 events, never ended, and the reading counts the 3 that did not end; 0 and 10001 sessions and 400 never-ending sessions (over 200000 events) are refused, as is a protocol with issues; 4000 weighted coin flips give heads within four standard deviations of 2000; the geometric loop gives exactly 4000 Tails and heads within four standard deviations of 4000; 20000 draws of a fair choice are fair and repeat about half the time", "LeanFM/MCPTests.lean: session_actor and session_dataset are listed; stdio runs both; the view's smoke test navigates to a role (asking session_actor for that role, drawing its diagram, its messages and the payloads and marking its button), clamps the dataset size to 10000, sends the size to session_dataset and draws paths, load, provenance and the walker sentence, shows the proto file and returns to the whole protocol"]
  , decision "session-chain-causes"
      ["clause:selfhost.session.generate.chain", "clause:selfhost.session.generative.monitor"]
